    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** Any two of: why, how, what, when, where, who.\n\n**2.** Please may I have the book?\n\n**3.** Good morning, sir.\n\n**4.** do not\n\n**5.** For example: Good morning. My name is …\n\n**6.** A well-known building or feature used as a guide.\n\n**7.** Quiet or soft.\n\n**8.** Any open question, for example: Why did you choose that school?\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** register\n\n**2.** slang\n\n**3.** jargon\n\n**4.** contracted\n\n**5.** open\n\n**6.** elaboration\n\n**7.** modulation\n\n**8.** A nod, a smile, a gesture or a look that carries meaning without any words being spoken.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) F (b) I (c) F (d) I\n\n**2.** Please may I have that form?\n\n**3.** Would you like to come?\n\n**4.** Come with me, please. (Any natural informal version.)\n\n**5.** For example: (a) What did you think of the book? (b) In what way does child labour harm a child? (c) How long did the journey take?\n\n**6.** For example: Yes, I did, because the ending explained everything that had puzzled me earlier.\n\n**7.** Any three permanent features: a church, a mosque, a filling station, a clinic, a school.\n\n**8.** Stress **she**.\n\n**9.** Any two of: pace (to give the listener time), volume (to be heard), tone (to show attitude), stress (to mark the important word).\n\n**10.** For example: Good morning, sir. You are most welcome to Nkwanta Junior High School.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** The formal version uses a greeting, complete sentences and a courtesy expression; the informal one uses a name, contracted forms and a shorter request.\n\n**2.** Any four beginning with why, how, what or in what way.\n\n**3.** For example: What can you see from where you are standing? Which direction are you facing?\n\n**4.** Stressing *I* means somebody else said it; stressing *say* suggests it was implied; stressing *she* means somebody else took it. Any three correct pairings are accepted.\n\n\n\n**5.** It gives no order, no distance and no landmark the stranger can identify. A correct version names the stages, the landmarks and the distance.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Register is the level of formality a speaker chooses. It is decided by the listener, the place, the purpose, and whether the words are being recorded — any two. **[3]**\n\n**2.** (a) Please may I have that book? (b) He cannot do it. **[3]**\n\n**3.** A closed question can be answered with yes, no or one word; an open question requires a fuller answer. Examples must match. **[4]**\n\n**4.** Marked for order, landmarks, distance and completeness. **[5]**\n\n**5.** Pace, volume, tone and stress, each with a correct purpose. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Make clear whose experience it was, and keep to what that person actually said or did rather than inventing their feelings. **[2]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: order of stages (2), at least three permanent landmarks (3), distance given in two ways (2), overshoot warning (1), full sentences and correct punctuation (2). A set of directions that would actually work is worth more than an elegant one that would not — read them and ask whether you could follow them yourself.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Register role-play.** Listen for the greeting, the complete sentences and the absence of contracted forms in the formal version. The class should be able to name two features unprompted.\n\n- **The interview.** Time it. If the interviewer speaks more than a quarter of the time, the questions were closed, whatever they look like on paper.\n\n- Sign the log only when the task has been performed aloud in front of somebody. A written answer is not a substitute.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Core 2 to 4.** Accept any natural rewriting. There is no single correct formal version, and learners should not be penalised for choosing a different courtesy expression.\n\n- **Core 8 and Challenge 4.** Stress is heard, not seen. Have these read aloud before they are marked.\n\n- **Directions.** The commonest fault is a landmark that will not last — a parked lorry, a hawker's table, a poster. Ask of each: will it be there next year?\n\n- **Open questions.** A question beginning with *do you* or *did you* is closed however long the rest of it is.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Oral Language (Listening and Speaking)*"
