    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** Register.\n\n**2.** Any question beginning with why, how or what.\n\n**3.** Informal.\n\n**4.** One thousand two hundred.\n\n**5.** A fuller answer giving reasons or examples.\n\n**6.** Pace, volume, tone or stress.\n\n**7.** govt\n\n**8.** The school has 420 pupils.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** message\n\n**2.** mood\n\n**3.** tone\n\n**4.** implicit\n\n**5.** inference\n\n**6.** fact\n\n**7.** opinion\n\n**8.** Tone is the speaker's attitude to the subject — angry, amused, doubtful. Volume is only how loud the voice is; a speaker can be furious in a whisper.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Marked on completeness: speaker, event, place and date, every figure, the reason and the action asked for.\n\n**2.** Marked for accuracy of figures and for the order of importance.\n\n**3.** (a) F (b) O (c) F (d) O\n\n**4.** (a) sarcastic or impatient (b) restrained, possibly resentful\n\n**5.** That payment is still expected — implied by *not yet* and *been able*.\n\n**6.** For example: Assembly asks parents — bring child health record books.\n\n**7.** Any three, for example govt (government), ↑ (a rise), ∴ (therefore), & (and), no. (number).\n\n**8.** Marked against the original: every figure correct.\n\n**9.** For example: Please could you repeat the figure for last year?\n\n**10.** The message is what the speaker wants you to take away; the mood is the feeling the talk creates in you.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Marked on the sorting: every claim attached to the speaker who made it.\n\n**2.** The difference must lie in the words chosen, not in the facts given.\n\n**3.** *A few* minimises the problem; the speaker is making it sound smaller than it may be.\n\n**4.** Tone is attitude, not volume. A correct answer names an attitude — impatient, respectful, sarcastic, anxious.\n\n**5.** Marked on whether the words quoted actually carry the implication claimed.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** The message is what you are meant to take away; the mood is what you feel; the tone is the speaker's attitude to the subject. **[4]**\n\n**2.** (a) F (b) O (c) F **[3]**\n\n**3.** That payment is still intended; *not yet* and *been able*. **[3]**\n\n**4.** Who, what, when and where, how many, why, so what. **[3]**\n\n**5.** A sentence can be rebuilt from memory afterwards; an exact number cannot. **[2]**\n\n**6.** That the speaker has reservations they are unwilling to state — implied by the guarded wording and by the refusal to continue. **[5]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: figures exact (3), claims attributed to speakers (2), report intelligible to somebody who did not hear the bulletin (2), opinion kept out of the report body (2), the writer's own view clearly signalled at the end (1). The commonest failure is a report that slides into opinion halfway through without saying so.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Above and below the line.** What appears below the line after the second hearing is the real evidence of listening. Expect facts to survive the first hearing and implied meaning not to.\n\n- **The group discussion.** Listen for learners who label their own statements. A learner who says *that is my opinion, not something the speaker said* has understood the chapter.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Tone is attitude, not volume.** This is the single most common error in listening questions and is worth correcting every time it appears.\n\n- **Figures.** Mark the note-taking on the figures first. A set of notes with the numbers wrong is worse than useless, because it looks reliable.\n\n- **Inference.** An inference must be tied to particular words. *I just think so* earns nothing.\n\n- **Core 1, 2 and 8** need a reader. They cannot be set as homework.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Oral Language (Listening and Speaking)*"
