    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** 26\n\n**2.** a, e, i, o, u\n\n**3.** More.\n\n**4.** Any of: bat, hat, mat, sat.\n\n**5.** The vowel in *sheep*.\n\n**6.** Any two of: think, three, thumb, thick.\n\n**7.** Slashes, / /.\n\n**8.** The stressed syllable.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** vowel\n\n**2.** pure\n\n**3.** diphthong\n\n**4.** minimal\n\n**5.** phonetic\n\n**6.** centring\n\n**7.** closing\n\n**8.** English spelling does not match its sounds one to one. The same letter stands for different sounds in different words, which is why sounds are written between slashes.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** For example: sit, bin; bed, ten; cat, bag; hot, dog.\n\n**2.** For example: sheep, feet; car, far; door, more; food, moon.\n\n**3.** sheep, feet, caught (or coat), fool\n\n**4.** Marked on the partner's transcription.\n\n**5.** seat, fool, caught\n\n**6.** For example: ear, air, sure, tour, pure.\n\n**7.** For example: day, buy, boy, go, cow.\n\n**8.** Long vowels in *old*, *boat*, *floated*, *slowly*, *deep*.\n\n**9.** short, long, short, long\n\n**10.** For example: The teacher agreed to meet the three of us at three.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Any correct pairs, for example ship/sheep, bit/beat, full/fool, cot/caught, pull/pool.\n\n**2.** In conversation the situation usually makes the meaning clear. In a dictation there is no situation to help, and the wrong word is simply wrong.\n\n**3.** A pure vowel keeps the same quality throughout — /iː/ in *see*, /ɑː/ in *car*. A diphthong glides — /eɪ/ in *day*, /aʊ/ in *cow*.\n\n**4.** It is a regular feature of Ghanaian English, which is a legitimate variety spoken by millions. The contrast is worth being able to make in an oral examination, a dictation, or with a listener unfamiliar with the accent.\n\n**5.** Any workable tongue twister; the sound must genuinely recur.\n\n\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** One letter can stand for several sounds and one sound can be spelt several ways — for example the u of *put* and of *but*. **[3]**\n\n**2.** Any correct examples, such as sit and bed; sheep and car. **[2]**\n\n**3.** sheep, fool, caught **[3]**\n\n**4.** A pure vowel keeps its quality; a diphthong glides. Examples must match. **[4]**\n\n**5.** Centring (ear, air) and closing (day, cow). **[4]**\n\n**6.** It is a feature of Ghanaian English rather than an error, but the contrast should be available for oral examinations and for listeners unfamiliar with the accent. **[4]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: length about eighty words (1), six long vowels correctly identified (3), six short vowels correctly identified (3), passage makes sense as a piece of writing (1), honest reflection on the writer's own pronunciation (2). Mark the underlining by ear, not by spelling — *bread* has a short vowel although it is spelt with ea.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The dictation.** Sitting back to back matters: lips must not be readable. Record both scores, and note which pairs the class as a whole found hardest.\n\n- **The reading aloud.** Mark the vowels only. Do not deduct for a Ghanaian accent; deduct only where a long vowel is shortened so far that the word changes.\n\n- Sign the log only when the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Spelling against sound.** Learners will mark vowels by letter rather than by sound. *Bread*, *head* and *said* all have short vowels despite their spelling.\n\n- **Diphthongs are one sound.** A learner who says *ee-uh* for *ear* has produced two vowels. Ask them to say it while sliding their jaw slowly.\n\n- **Respect.** The Ghanaian English point in Challenge 4 and Exam 6 is deliberate and should be taught as written. Learners are being given a second register, not corrected out of their own.\n\n- **Core 4 and the speaking tasks** need a partner and cannot be set as written homework.\n\n\n\n### Review 1 — Oral Language (Listening and Speaking)\n\n*Chapters 1 to 3. Time: 40 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** The level of formality a speaker chooses; decided by the listener, the place, the purpose or whether the words are recorded. **[3]**\n\n**2.** Please may I have that form? / Would you like to come? **[3]**\n\n**3.** Any versions beginning with what, why or how. **[3]**\n\n**4.** Order, landmarks, distance, and an overshoot warning. **[5]**\n\n**5.** Message: what you are meant to take away. Mood: what you feel. Tone: the speaker's attitude. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Who, what, when and where, how many, why, so what. **[3]**\n\n**7.** (a) F (b) O **[2]**\n\n**8.** sheep, fool, caught **[3]**\n\n**9.** A pure vowel keeps its quality; a diphthong glides. **[3]**\n\n**10.** Pace, volume, tone and stress. **[2]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 2: Reading*"
