    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** The topic sentence.\n\n**2.** Cash crops.\n\n**3.** Vehicles.\n\n**4.** Quotation marks.\n\n**5.** The man was exhausted.\n\n**6.** To give the main points in a much shorter form.\n\n**7.** Six.\n\n**8.** Now.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** central or main\n\n**2.** supporting\n\n**3.** aside\n\n**4.** redundancy\n\n**5.** generic\n\n**6.** paraphrase\n\n**7.** evidence\n\n**8.** A paraphrase restates a passage at about the same length; a summary keeps only what is essential and is much shorter.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** One sentence per paragraph.\n\n**2.** (a) cereals (b) health workers (c) vehicles\n\n**3.** gift, returned, in my opinion\n\n**4.** For example: Learning a trade means learning to judge as well as to do.\n\n**5.** For example: We cannot pay now.\n\n**6.** For example: Assembly asks parents to bring child health record books.\n\n**7.** Marked on the count and on whether the central idea survived.\n\n**8.** The claim plus an exact quotation from the text and a word naming what carries the meaning.\n\n**9.** Exact wording, quotation marks, and a location.\n\n**10.** A summary reports what the writer said. An opinion added to it misleads the reader about what was in the original.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** The sentence surviving to the last stage should be the text's central idea.\n\n**2.** Only one word has been altered, and altered wrongly. A paraphrase changes the structure of the sentence, not a single word.\n\n**3.** Marked on the count and on whether the meaning survived.\n\n**4.** The limit tests selection, which is the skill being examined. Beyond the limit the examiner stops reading, so anything important placed after it is lost.\n\n**5.** The quotation must actually support the claim named.\n\n\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** The one thing the paragraph is chiefly saying; test by asking whether every other sentence supports it. **[3]**\n\n**2.** (a) cereals (b) health workers **[2]**\n\n**3.** Examples, repetition, asides — also restatement. **[3]**\n\n**4.** Any recasting that changes the structure and keeps the meaning. **[4]**\n\n**5.** Marked on the count, the central idea, and the absence of the summariser's opinion. **[5]**\n\n**6.** Without them it is only the reader's impression; the quotation is what makes it an answer about the text. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: within the ninety-word limit with the count given (2), central idea present (2), proportions of the original kept (2), own words rather than lifted phrases (2), opinion kept out of the summary and clearly labelled separately (2). A summary over the limit is marked only as far as the limit.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The one-minute summary.** The test is whether the listeners can state the subject afterwards. If they cannot, the summary reported details rather than the central idea.\n\n- **Paraphrasing aloud.** *Merely reworded* means one or two synonyms swapped. Insist on a different sentence structure.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Word counts.** Require the number to be written. It takes ten seconds and it is what examiners do.\n\n- **Concessions.** A summary that drops the writer's admissions has changed the argument. This is worth a mark of its own.\n\n- **Lifting.** Copying a phrase from the passage into the summary is the commonest fault. Ask learners to close the book before writing the sentence.\n\n- **Exam 5** needs a passage supplied by the teacher; choose one of about two hundred words.\n\n\n\n### Review 2 — Reading\n\n*Chapters 4 and 5. Time: 40 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** A purpose is what you intend to get from the text; scanning for a fact, skimming for the sense, close reading to explain. **[4]**\n\n**2.** Skimming reads quickly for general sense; scanning searches for one particular fact. **[3]**\n\n**3.** A blank page, an unattached pronoun, a sentence that does not follow, a wandering mind. **[4]**\n\n**4.** For example: use the context for an unknown word; skim back to the last heading. **[4]**\n\n**5.** The one thing the paragraph is chiefly saying; test whether every other sentence supports it. **[3]**\n\n**6.** (a) cereals (b) health workers **[2]**\n\n**7.** Examples, repetition, asides, restatement — any three. **[3]**\n\n**8.** Any recasting that changes the structure. **[3]**\n\n**9.** Without them it is only the reader's impression. **[2]**\n\n**10.** Marked on count, central idea and absence of opinion. **[2]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Grammar Usage*"
