    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** A noun.\n\n**2.** children\n\n**3.** went\n\n**4.** An adjective.\n\n**5.** on (or under, beside…)\n\n**6.** but (He was tired but he finished the work.)\n\n**7.** Ama said she was ready.\n\n**8.** quickly\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** noun\n\n**2.** pronoun\n\n**3.** adjective\n\n**4.** verb\n\n**5.** adverb\n\n**6.** preposition\n\n**7.** conjunction\n\n**8.** A word's class comes from the job it does in the sentence. *Water* is a noun in *the water is cold* and a verb in *water the plants*.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) noun (b) adverb (c) adjective (d) preposition (or conjunction, if a clause follows)\n\n**2.** (a) noun (b) verb\n\n**3.** a large old wooden door\n\n**4.** a beautiful white cotton dress\n\n**5.** counted, put, walked\n\n**6.** I\n\n**7.** me\n\n**8.** carefully, happily, quickly, gently\n\n**9.** at, until, in (or similar sensible choices)\n\n**10.** (a) but (b) but (or: although we waited, the bus did not come)\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** For example: (a) He bought a round of drinks. (b) The road rounds the hill. (c) a round table (d) She walked round the corner.\n\n**2.** The pronoun is a subject, so it must be *I*. Test by removing the other person: *me went* is clearly wrong, *I went* is right.\n\n**3.** For example: Kofi told Yaw that Yaw's bicycle had been stolen.\n\n**4.** Any correct sentence with all seven labelled.\n\n**5.** English fixes the order of adjectives: opinion, size, then colour. The correct order is *a beautiful big red cloth*.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** Noun, pronoun, adjective, verb, adverb, preposition, conjunction, interjection. **[4]**\n\n**2.** (a) noun (b) adverb (c) preposition **[3]**\n\n**3.** a large old wooden door **[3]**\n\n**4.** *I*, because the pronoun is the subject of the verb. **[3]**\n\n**5.** carefully, happily, gently, plus a correct sentence. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Any correct pair. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: length (1), at least eight adjectives used well (3), at least four adverbs (2), three prepositions (1), marking of the word classes accurate (2), description actually describes — the reader can see the place (1). Deduct for strings of four or more adjectives before a single noun.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The description game.** Listen for adjective order. A learner who says *a red big cloth* will be understood but should be asked to say it again.\n\n- **The word-class game.** Ten seconds is deliberately tight; the point is that class comes from the job, which learners can hear faster than they can explain.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Class comes from the job.** The whole chapter rests on this. A learner who asks *what is this word?* rather than *what is it doing?* will get every borderline case wrong.\n\n- **Pronoun case.** The removal test — take the other person out — settles *Ama and I* against *Ama and me* every time.\n\n- **Adjective order** is felt rather than learnt. Reading aloud is the quickest correction.\n\n- **Core 1(d).** *After* is a preposition before a noun and a conjunction before a clause. Accept either with a correct reason.\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Grammar Usage*"
