    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** saw\n\n**2.** written\n\n**3.** will (shall)\n\n**4.** We are not told.\n\n**5.** aren't you\n\n**6.** has\n\n**7.** were marked by the teacher\n\n**8.** Negative.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** conditional\n\n**2.** if or conditional\n\n**3.** main\n\n**4.** active\n\n**5.** passive\n\n**6.** agent\n\n**7.** tag\n\n**8.** The tag asks the listener to confirm the opposite possibility, so it turns the polarity round: *She is late, isn't she?*\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) zero (b) first (c) second\n\n**2.** rains, will stay\n\n**3.** had, would ride\n\n**4.** Any correct first conditional.\n\n**5.** (a) The letter was signed by the head. (b) The drains have been cleared.\n\n**6.** For example: Somebody broke the window during the night.\n\n**7.** (a) aren't you? (b) has she? (c) didn't they?\n\n**8.** (a) aren't I? (b) shall we? (c) will you?\n\n**9.** did they?\n\n**10.** (a) rising (b) falling\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Three correct sentences, each of the right type.\n\n**2.** *Would* belongs only in the main clause. The if-clause takes *had*: *If I had known, I would have come.*\n\n**3.** For example: The headteacher has decided to lock the gate at six. The watchman will not admit latecomers.\n\n**4.** The doer is not named. For example: *The clerk made two mistakes in the register.*\n\n**5.** Any natural conversation with correct tags and sensible marking.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Zero (always true), first (real future possibility), second (unreal or unlikely). **[4]**\n\n**2.** had, would ride **[2]**\n\n\n\n**3.** (a) The letter was signed by the head. (b) The drains have been cleared. **[4]**\n\n**4.** When the doer is unknown, obvious or unimportant; when the receiver is the real subject of the paragraph; in formal or scientific writing. **[3]**\n\n**5.** (a) aren't I? (b) did they? (c) shall we? **[4]**\n\n**6.** A rising tag is a real question; a falling tag expects agreement. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: length (1), four passives correctly formed (4), two agents named (1), two agentless passives with a written reason for each (3), the report is coherent (1). The marginal explanations are the heart of the task: a learner who cannot say why the doer was left out has used the passive by accident.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Tag tennis.** The second round is the assessment: *I am* → *aren't I*, *let us* → *shall we*, *nobody* → *did they*, imperatives → *will you*.\n\n- **Rising against falling.** The class should hear the difference before anybody explains it. If they cannot, have the pair exaggerate both versions.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Would in the if-clause** is the defining conditional error. It appears in speech constantly and must be corrected in writing.\n\n- **Passive by accident.** Many learners write passives without noticing. The marginal explanation in the writing task is designed to expose this.\n\n- **Tags.** Four cases carry most of the marks: *I am*, imperatives, *let us*, and statements containing a negative word such as *nobody* or *hardly*.\n\n- **Intonation** cannot be marked from paper. Use the speaking log.\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Grammar Usage*"
