    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** Large (or huge, enormous).\n\n**2.** Dishonest.\n\n**3.** impossible\n\n**4.** happiness\n\n**5.** The part of speech, the pronunciation, and an example sentence.\n\n**6.** Heavy.\n\n**7.** happy\n\n**8.** Reduce (or decrease, shrink).\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** synonym\n\n**2.** antonym\n\n**3.** prefix\n\n**4.** suffix\n\n**5.** root\n\n**6.** collocations\n\n**7.** context\n\n**8.** They usually differ in strength, in formality, or in the approval they carry: *thrifty* and *stingy* describe the same habit quite differently.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** For example: large, rapid, cheerful, hard.\n\n**2.** modern, mean, permanent, decrease\n\n**3.** illegal, immature, irregular, dishonest, irrelevant\n\n**4.** government, happiness, decision, arrival\n\n**5.** transport, import, export, portable (also porter, deport).\n\n**6.** heavy rain; make a mistake; hard work\n\n**7.** Moribund means dying or no longer active.\n\n**8.** purchase, request, conclude, obtain\n\n**9.** *Industrial* relates to factories and industry; *industrious* means hard-working.\n\n**10.** Marked on completeness of the record.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** displeased, annoyed, irritated, furious, enraged\n\n**2.** thrifty / stingy; assured / arrogant; eloquent / garrulous — any correct pair.\n\n**3.** Every word has been replaced by a more formal one that does not suit the occasion. Formality must match the situation, and here it does not.\n\n**4.** predict, dictate, dictionary, contradict, verdict — any four.\n\n**5.** Marked on all four parts being present.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** For example: ancient — old / modern; generous — giving / mean. **[4]**\n\n**2.** illegal, immature, irregular, irrelevant **[4]**\n\n**3.** transport, import, export, portable **[3]**\n\n**4.** *Heavy rain*. English fixes which words go together, and *strong rain* is understood but not said. **[3]**\n\n**5.** *Industrial* relates to industry and factories; *industrious* means hard-working. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Register must match the occasion. Words chosen only for their length draw attention to themselves and away from the meaning. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: length (1), twelve less common words used and underlined (4), each word actually suiting its sentence (3), the closing list of three with synonyms (2). Deduct where a learner has emptied a thesaurus into the page: a word used wrongly is worth less than the ordinary word it replaced.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The connotation line.** The disagreements are the interesting part. Connotation is not fixed in a dictionary; it is what a community of speakers has come to feel.\n\n- **The word-family game.** Listen for learners who produce a word from the right family but the wrong meaning — *industrial* for *industrious* is the standard case.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Thesaurus damage.** The commonest fault in this chapter is a description in which every word has been swapped for a longer one. Mark for suitability, not for rarity.\n\n- **Collocation** cannot be reasoned out; it must be met in reading. Keep a class list on the wall.\n\n- **Prefixes.** *il-* before l, *im-* before m and p, *ir-* before r, *in-* elsewhere. Learners who know this rule stop guessing.\n\n- **Core 10 and Challenge 5** require a dictionary; make sure one is available.\n\n\n\n*Strand 3: Grammar Usage*"
