    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** A simile.\n\n**2.** bee\n\n**3.** A proverb.\n\n**4.** Any correct proverb.\n\n**5.** It is raining very heavily.\n\n**6.** An idiom.\n\n**7.** Figurative.\n\n**8.** nine\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** proverb\n\n**2.** simile\n\n**3.** metaphor\n\n**4.** personification\n\n**5.** idiom\n\n**6.** hyperbole\n\n**7.** figurative\n\n**8.** It carries the authority of many speakers before you, and it puts a general truth in a form short enough to remember and repeat.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Trouble is shared; you are not the only one suffering.\n\n**2.** Dealing with a small problem at once prevents a large one later.\n\n**3.** When circumstances change, behaviour must change with them.\n\n**4.** (a) Many hands make light work / no man is an island. (b) If you run after two hares you will catch neither.\n\n**5.** (a) simile (b) metaphor (c) personification (d) hyperbole\n\n**6.** Any two workable similes.\n\n**7.** Any two workable metaphors.\n\n**8.** To refuse to take sides.\n\n**9.** To avoid coming to the point.\n\n**10.** Any correct use, with the proverb suiting the situation.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Three proverbs, each with a meaning and an occasion.\n\n**2.** Personification gives the river an attitude, and indifference is exactly the point being made; the plain version states a fact and carries no feeling.\n\n**3.** Three correctly labelled figures in a coherent paragraph.\n\n**4.** A comparison must bring in something different. Comparing a thing to itself adds nothing, and *busy like a busy place* is empty repetition.\n\n**5.** Too many comparisons crowd each other out, the reader stops believing them, and none is remembered. One image that earns its place is remembered because it stands alone.\n\n\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Trouble is shared; you are not the only one affected. **[3]**\n\n**2.** (a) simile (b) metaphor (c) personification **[3]**\n\n**3.** A simile compares using *like* or *as*; a metaphor states the comparison outright. **[4]**\n\n**4.** To refuse to take sides, plus a correct sentence. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Any two workable figures. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Too many crowd each other out and the reader stops believing them; one is remembered because it stands alone. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: length (1), a proverb that genuinely fits the situation (3), one simile, one metaphor and one personification (3), correct labelling (2), the piece reads as writing rather than as a list of devices (1). A proverb dropped in for its own sake earns nothing, however well quoted.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The story ending in a proverb.** The test is whether the class can say why it fits. If they cannot, the proverb was ornament rather than conclusion.\n\n- **The proverb exchange.** Learners may give proverbs in Twi, Ewe, Ga, Dagbani or any Ghanaian language; the English explanation is what is being assessed.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Three parts to a proverb answer:** the literal picture, the meaning, the occasion. Most learners give only the second.\n\n- **Ghanaian proverbs are an asset here.** Learners often know dozens and do not think of them as school knowledge. This chapter is where that changes.\n\n- **Overloading.** Watch for paragraphs stuffed with similes. One good image beats five.\n\n- **Challenge 1** is a home task and works best if learners are told to write the proverb down in the original language as well as in English.\n\n\n\n### Review 3 — Grammar Usage\n\n*Chapters 6 to 11. Time: 50 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** noun, adverb, preposition **[3]**\n\n**2.** a large old wooden door **[2]**\n\n**3.** Subject: The tall man in the blue overall. Predicate: repaired the pump. **[3]**\n\n**4.** simple, compound, complex **[3]**\n\n**5.** A comma splice; repair with a full stop, a semi-colon, or a comma plus *but*. **[4]**\n\n**6.** had, would ride **[2]**\n\n**7.** The letter was signed by the head. **[3]**\n\n**8.** aren't I? did they? shall we? **[3]**\n\n**9.** the girls' uniforms; the children's books **[3]**\n\n**10.** Trouble is shared; personification. **[4]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing*"
