    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** At the top right.\n\n**2.** Dear (name),\n\n**3.** Yours sincerely, or Your friend, or Love.\n\n**4.** A comma.\n\n**5.** For example: 14 March 2026.\n\n**6.** A heading, the date, or the writer's name.\n\n**7.** Dr\n\n**8.** A registration form (or health record).\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** formal\n\n**2.** informal\n\n**3.** salutation\n\n**4.** subscription\n\n**5.** heading\n\n**6.** notice\n\n**7.** *Yours faithfully* follows *Dear Sir* or *Dear Madam*, when the person is not named. *Yours sincerely* follows a named person, such as *Dear Mr Asare*.\n\n**8.** underlined\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) Dear Uncle, … Yours sincerely / Your nephew. (b) Dear Sir/Madam, … Yours faithfully.\n\n**2.** (a) right (b) right, under your address (c) left\n\n**3.** For example: DAMAGE TO A DESK IN FORM 1B — underlined.\n\n**4.** One paragraph stating who is writing and what is wanted.\n\n**5.** A specific request with a deadline.\n\n**6.** Heading, body, writer's name and office, date.\n\n**7.** Any short memorable headline and slogan.\n\n**8.** A headline, a short description, the benefit or offer, and contact details or the venue and time.\n\n**9.** Name, age or date of birth, address, next of kin, telephone number.\n\n**10.** For example: I am writing about the condition of the 7 a.m. service of 8 March, which caused damage to my luggage.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** All eight parts present, correct subscription, one clear request with a date.\n\n**2.** Each matter may go to a different person or file; a letter carrying two is answered on one and forgotten on the other.\n\n**3.** The rewrite must add the day, the time, the place and what to bring.\n\n**4.** Headline, benefit, contact, and a stated audience.\n\n**5.** Anger lets the reader dismiss the writer instead of the claim. Dates, costs and a specific request leave nothing to dismiss.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** Sender's address (top right), date, recipient's address (left), salutation, heading, body, subscription, signature and printed name. **[4]**\n\n**2.** *Yours faithfully* after *Dear Sir/Madam*; *Yours sincerely* after a named person. **[3]**\n\n**3.** Dear Sir/Madam, … Yours faithfully. **[2]**\n\n**4.** An underlined heading and one clear opening paragraph. **[4]**\n\n**5.** Heading, body, name, office and date. **[4]**\n\n**6.** A factual opening naming the service, the date and the problem. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: all eight parts correctly placed (3), correct subscription for the salutation (1), the facts dated and costed (2), one specific remedy asked for with a deadline (2), tone firm and factual throughout (2). A letter that asks for nothing is filed, however well written, and should be marked accordingly.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Reading the letter to its recipient.** The question *would you act on this?* is the best test there is of a letter, and learners answer it honestly about each other's work.\n\n- **The unwilling official.** Two minutes is long enough for courtesy to slip. Listen for the learner who stays polite and keeps returning to the request.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Layout carries marks before content.** Mark the eight parts first, every time; learners learn quickly that the form is not optional.\n\n- **One purpose per letter.** Challenge 2 makes the point, and it applies to every letter learners will write as adults.\n\n- **Ask for something specific.** *Please do something about it* is not a request. A named remedy with a date is.\n\n- **Real complaints.** Where a learner's letter concerns something genuine, encourage them to send it and record any reply. Nothing teaches this chapter faster.\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing*"
