    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** A dictionary.\n\n**2.** An encyclopaedia.\n\n**3.** The district assembly, a library, a newspaper, or an elder.\n\n**4.** Stealing their work.\n\n**5.** Any question beginning with why, how or what.\n\n**6.** Notes.\n\n**7.** A title (and labelled axes).\n\n**8.** For example: According to the district health office, …\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** research\n\n**2.** focused\n\n**3.** primary\n\n**4.** secondary\n\n**5.** acknowledging or citing\n\n**6.** plagiarism\n\n**7.** bibliography or reference list\n\n**8.** It is too broad and it asks for an opinion rather than evidence. A researchable question names something that can be counted, observed or asked about.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Three focused questions naming something countable or askable.\n\n**2.** (a) primary (b) secondary (c) primary (d) secondary\n\n**3.** (a) meteorological records (b) a survey of the class (c) an elder, or a local history\n\n**4.** Three open questions.\n\n**5.** For example: erosion caused by water running over the ground, not soaking in.\n\n**6.** Author, title, publisher, year, and the page.\n\n**7.** For example: According to *The District Herald* of 3 May, …\n\n**8.** (c), (b), (a), (d)\n\n**9.** Finding and interpretation clearly separated.\n\n**10.** No named author or date; no evidence given; the writer stands to gain from being believed; it cannot be checked anywhere else.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Five neutral questions; a leading question loses the mark.\n\n**2.** A finding is what the data shows; an opinion is what you make of it. Both are needed but they must be labelled.\n\n**3.** It is plagiarism. The sentences should have been put in the learner's own words, or quoted with quotation marks and the source named.\n\n**4.** Marked on whether the second source genuinely bears on the same fact.\n\n**5.** Four references with enough detail for somebody else to find the source.\n\n\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** It must be narrow enough that you can name the information that would answer it. Any suitably narrowed question is accepted. **[4]**\n\n**2.** (a) primary (b) secondary (c) primary **[3]**\n\n**3.** No named author or date; no evidence; an interest in being believed; cannot be checked. **[3]**\n\n**4.** Presenting another person's words or ideas as your own. Avoid it by writing in your own words or by quoting and naming the source. **[4]**\n\n**5.** Question, method, findings, meaning. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Two labelled sentences, correctly distinguished. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: a genuinely focused question (2), method stated including whom and how many (2), findings given with figures (2), conclusions kept separate from findings (2), all sources named (2). A report with no method cannot be trusted, however interesting its findings.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The three-minute presentation.** Listen for the method. Learners give findings readily and skip how they got them, which is the part that makes findings worth anything.\n\n- **The interview.** Six open questions in five minutes means the adult should be speaking for most of it. Time it if necessary.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Narrow the question first.** Most weak projects are weak because the question was never narrowed. Collect the questions before any research begins.\n\n- **Findings against conclusions.** Requiring the two to be labelled separately is the single most useful discipline in this chapter.\n\n- **Plagiarism.** Treat it as a habit to be built rather than an offence to be punished at this stage: close the book, then write.\n\n- **Challenge 1 and the writing task** need a lesson between them for the data to be collected.\n\n\n\n### Review 4 — Writing\n\n*Chapters 12 to 15. Time: 45 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** A group of sentences developing one idea; one only. **[2]**\n\n**2.** Planning, drafting, revising, editing, publishing. **[3]**\n\n**3.** Revising changes content and order; editing corrects surface errors. **[3]**\n\n**4.** Narrative, descriptive, informational, persuasive, each with its purpose. **[4]**\n\n**5.** Any rewriting with precise detail. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Sender's address, date, recipient's address, salutation, heading, body, subscription, signature and name. **[4]**\n\n**7.** Faithfully after Dear Sir/Madam; sincerely after a named person. **[3]**\n\n**8.** Heading, body, name and date. **[3]**\n\n**9.** It must name information you can actually gather. **[3]**\n\n**10.** Presenting another's words or ideas as your own; avoid by rewriting in your own words or by quoting and naming the source. **[2]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 5: Literature*"
