    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** The main character or protagonist.\n\n**2.** The setting.\n\n**3.** The plot.\n\n**4.** A simile.\n\n**5.** Dialogue.\n\n**6.** It varies — four is common.\n\n**7.** Any correct answer, such as Kweku Ananse.\n\n**8.** Rhyme.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** characters\n\n**2.** setting\n\n**3.** plot\n\n**4.** theme\n\n**5.** direction\n\n**6.** stanza\n\n**7.** scheme\n\n**8.** The subject is what happens — a boy loses a goat. The theme is the idea about life that survives when the events are taken away — for example that carelessness costs other people.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Characters, setting, plot, theme (also point of view, conflict).\n\n**2.** A character plus evidence, not an adjective alone.\n\n**3.** Both place and time.\n\n**4.** exposition, rising action, climax, resolution\n\n**5.** A sentence about life, not a single word.\n\n**6.** Names on the left with a colon, a new line for each speaker, stage directions in brackets.\n\n**7.** Correct script conventions.\n\n**8.** Lines and stanzas, rhythm, rhyme or its absence, imagery, repetition.\n\n**9.** The image quoted and its effect named.\n\n**10.** A story can tell the reader directly. A play must show it through what the character says and does, or through a speech made alone on stage.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** The reader should be able to say what the character is like without being told.\n\n**2.** All five stages identified from the same story.\n\n**3.** Correct script form, plus the observation that thoughts cannot be narrated in a play.\n\n**4.** Four lines with the marked scheme genuinely followed.\n\n**5.** Personification gives the river an attitude — indifference — which is the point being made; the plain sentence states a fact and carries no feeling.\n\n\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Characters, setting, plot, theme. **[3]**\n\n**2.** exposition, rising action, climax, resolution **[3]**\n\n**3.** The subject is what happens; the theme is the idea about life the story carries beyond its events. **[4]**\n\n**4.** Names on the left with colons, a new line for each speaker, stage directions in brackets. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Correct conventions throughout. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Through action, speech and the reactions of others — an example must be given. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: length (1), two characters and a clear setting (2), a plot with a genuine turning point (3), character shown through action and speech rather than adjectives (3), something changed by the end (1). The commonest failure is a sequence of events with nothing at stake — ask what is different on the last line from the first.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Telling the story aloud.** The class should be able to name what changed. If they cannot, the story has events but no plot.\n\n- **Performing the dialogue.** Stage directions must be obeyed, not read out. A learner who says *(quietly)* aloud has misunderstood the convention.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Show, do not tell.** The whole chapter rests on this. Learners will write *she was kind* and believe they have described a character.\n\n- **Theme as a sentence.** A word is a topic; a sentence is a theme. Insist on the sentence.\n\n- **Ghanaian material.** Folktales the learners already know are the best material for plot analysis, and they arrive with the class rather than having to be taught.\n\n- **Challenge 4.** The rhyme scheme must genuinely be abab; accept near-rhymes generously at this level.\n\n\n\n### Review 5 — Literature\n\n*Chapter 16. Time: 30 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** Characters, setting, plot, theme. **[4]**\n\n**2.** exposition, rising action, climax, resolution **[3]**\n\n**3.** The subject is what happens; the theme is the idea about life carried beyond the events. **[4]**\n\n**4.** Names with colons, a new line for each speaker, stage directions in brackets. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Correct conventions. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Stanzas, rhythm, rhyme, imagery, repetition — any three. **[3]**\n\n**7.** Through action, speech and the reactions of others. **[4]**\n\n**8.** Personification gives the river an indifference that is the point being made. **[3]**\n\n**9.** A sentence about life, not a single word. **[2]**"
